@page "/learn/ssr-client-validation"
@layout LearnLayout

<PageTitle>Client-side validation · Field Guide</PageTitle>

<article class="l-article" data-learn>
    <h1 class="l-title">Client-side validation</h1>
    <p class="l-sub">Someone types a bad email into a static form. Why does it take a trip to the server to say so?</p>
    <p class="l-meta">.NET 11 · Forms · Static SSR · about 5 minutes</p>
    <p class="l-new">
        <b>What's new in .NET 11:</b> a static form checks its rules in the browser before it posts, with no code
        changes. Static forms came in .NET 8, and up to .NET 10 only the server could check them, after a post.
    </p>

    <p>
        Below is a <i>New supplier</i> form. It's static SSR: the server sends plain HTML, with no live connection and
        no WebAssembly. <b>Company</b> is <code>[Required]</code>, and <b>Email</b> is
        <code>[Required, EmailAddress]</code>. Under the heading, the form counts how many posts reach the server.
    </p>
    <p>
        This first form behaves like a .NET 10 form. It runs on .NET 11 with the browser check turned off (Fig. 5
        shows how).
    </p>

    <DemoFigure Number="1" Title="A static form, checked on the server" Url="demo/client-validation/suppliers/new/net10"
                Label=".NET 10: checked on the server" Side="before">
        <Caption>
            Type <code>sales@@</code> into <b>Email</b> and press Tab: nothing happens. Press <b>Save supplier</b>.
            Both errors come back from the server, and <b>Posts to the server</b> goes to 1. The page doesn't flash,
            so that count is the only sign the form made a trip.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <h2><small>The problem</small>The page doesn't know the rules</h2>
    <p>Here's the email input the server sends, as .NET 10 renders it:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">The rendered input</span>
        <pre class="snip">&lt;input id="email" name="Input.Email" class="valid" /&gt;
<span class="err">no required, no data-val-*, no rules anywhere in the page</span></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        (<code>class="valid"</code> isn't a rule. Blazor adds it to any field with no errors yet.)
    </p>
    <p>
        <code>&lt;DataAnnotationsValidator /&gt;</code> runs on the server, when the form posts. The HTML says nothing
        about the rules, so the browser has nothing to check. The user hears nothing until they press Save. Then
        every mistake costs a round trip: send the form, check it, redraw the whole page. Enhanced navigation (the
        <code>Enhance</code> parameter) avoids a full page flash, but the round trip still happens. On a busy public form or a slow phone, it adds up.
    </p>

    <h2><small>The fix</small>Nothing to add</h2>
    <p>You write the same form as on .NET 10. What changes is the HTML it produces:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">The whole fix: no new code, on .NET 11</span>
        <pre class="snip"><span class="dim">&lt;!-- you write: unchanged from .NET 10 --&gt;</span>
&lt;EditForm Model="Input" FormName="new-supplier" OnValidSubmit="SaveAsync" Enhance&gt;
    &lt;DataAnnotationsValidator /&gt;
    ...
&lt;/EditForm&gt;

<span class="dim">&lt;!-- .NET 11 renders, for you, at the end of the form --&gt;</span>
<span class="ok">&lt;blazor-client-validation-data data-rules="…"&gt;</span></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        In .NET 11, <code>DataAnnotationsValidator</code> on a static form also writes the model's rules into the page.
        <code>blazor.web.js</code>, the script every Blazor Web App already loads, reads them. It checks each field as
        you leave it, and blocks a submit that has errors.
    </p>

    <DemoFigure Number="2" Title="The same form, checked in the browser" Url="demo/client-validation/suppliers/new"
                Label=".NET 11: checked in the browser">
        <Caption>
            Type <code>sales@@</code> into <b>Email</b> and press Tab: an error appears at once. Press
            <b>Save supplier</b>: both fields are flagged, and <b>Posts to the server</b> stays at 0. The browser
            stopped the post.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <p>
        Both demos share one model, and their pages differ by one parameter. Fig. 3 and Fig. 4 highlight code you
        already have, where the new behaviour comes from; the only new API, in Fig. 5, turns it off.
    </p>

    <CodeFigure Number="3" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/ClientValidation/NewSupplierForm.cs" Feature="ssr-client-validation">
        <Caption>
            The model both demos post. Its attributes are the rules, in the browser and on the server.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <CodeFigure Number="4" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/ClientValidation/NewSupplier.razor" Feature="ssr-client-validation">
        <Caption>
            The page in Fig. 2: a plain static form. The validator line hasn't changed. On .NET 11 it also writes the
            rules into the page.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <CodeFigure Number="5" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/ClientValidation/NewSupplierNet10.razor" Feature="ssr-client-validation">
        <Caption>
            The page in Fig. 1: the same form, with <code>DisableClientValidation="true"</code> (new in .NET 11) to
            get .NET 10's behaviour back. <code>Enhance</code> and the hidden <b>Posts</b> field are on both pages and
            don't affect this.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <h2><small>The .NET 10 way</small>Pick a cost</h2>
    <p>To catch mistakes before a post, a .NET 10 app had four options:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">Four ways to check a static form in .NET 10</span>
        <div class="flow">
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">&lt;DataAnnotationsValidator /&gt;</pre>
                <span class="why">let the server catch it, as Fig. 1 does</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ every mistake costs a post and a redraw of the whole page</div>
            </div>
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip"><span class="err">@@rendermode InteractiveServer</span></pre>
                <span class="why">make the form interactive</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ every visitor holds a live server connection open, just to hear "this field is required"</div>
            </div>
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">&lt;InputText ... <span class="err">required type="email"</span> /&gt;</pre>
                <span class="why">add HTML5 attributes to the inputs by hand</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ every rule written twice, only rules HTML supports, and the browser's wording instead of yours</div>
            </div>
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">&lt;script src="jquery.validate.unobtrusive.js"&gt;
&lt;InputText ... <span class="err">data-val-email="Email isn't valid."</span> /&gt;</pre>
                <span class="why">add jQuery Unobtrusive Validation</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ three more libraries, and every rule written twice: in C# and in the markup</div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <h2><small>.NET 11</small>The browser checks the same rules</h2>
    <p>
        In .NET 11, every static form with a <code>DataAnnotationsValidator</code> gets browser checks, built from
        the same attributes, with the same messages. It's on by default. To keep one form server-only, add
        <code>DisableClientValidation="true"</code> to its validator, as Fig. 5 does. To turn it off for the whole
        app, use <code>RazorComponentsServiceOptions.DisableClientValidation</code>.
    </p>

    <h2><small>Good to know</small></h2>
    <ul class="gk">
        <li><b>The server still checks every post.</b> The browser check is a convenience. With JavaScript off, or a button marked <code>formnovalidate</code>, the form posts and the server catches the mistake. Async rules, like "is this code taken?", only run on the server.</li>
        <li><b>Inputs inside your own wrapper components aren't checked in the browser.</b> If your inputs sit inside a component like <code>&lt;FormField&gt;</code>, as in many business apps, none of them gets a browser check. The form writes its rules when it first renders, and the wrapped inputs render after that, so their rules never make it into the page. The server still checks them.</li>
        <li><b>A blank optional email passes in the browser but fails on the server.</b> The browser lets it through, then the server rejects it after the post. That's because a static form posts an empty field as <code>""</code>, and <code>[EmailAddress]</code>, <code>[Phone]</code> and <code>[Url]</code> have always rejected <code>""</code>. Only the browser pass is new. Fix it by turning <code>""</code> into <code>null</code> in the property's setter.</li>
        <li><b>The browser shows the server's messages, translations included.</b> The server writes each message into the page, so messages from <a href="learn/validation-localization">Translated validation messages</a> show in the browser too.</li>
        <li><b>Your own attributes can have browser checks too.</b> Implement <code>IClientValidationRuleProvider</code> on the attribute, then register a matching JS function with <code>Blazor.formValidation.addValidator</code>.</li>
    </ul>
</article>
